1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,717
Book Four, Proposition Eight.

2
00:00:02,717 --> 00:00:05,806
In a given square to inscribe a circle.

3
00:00:05,806 --> 00:00:13,490
Let A B C D be the given square; it is required to inscribe a circle in the given square A B C D.

4
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:20,302
Bisect the sides A D and A B at the points E and F respectively by One Ten.

5
00:00:20,302 --> 00:00:31,040
Through E draw E H parallel to A B or C D, and through F draw F K parallel to A D or B C by One Thirty-One, intersecting at G.

6
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:39,224
Each of the resulting sub-figures is a parallelogram, so their opposite sides are equal by One Thirty-Four.

7
00:00:39,224 --> 00:00:55,114
Because A D equals A B, their halves A E and A F are equal, making the opposite sides F G and G E equal; and similarly G H and G K equal F G and G E, so all four segments G E, G F, G H, and G K are equal to one another.

8
00:00:55,114 --> 00:01:15,865
Therefore the circle described with center G and radius G E passes through F, H, and K; and because the angles at E, F, H, and K are right, by Three Sixteen the circle cannot cut the sides of the square, lest a line at right angles to a diameter at its extremity fall within the circle.

9
00:01:15,865 --> 00:01:24,136
Thus the circle touches all four sides A B, B C, C D, and D A, and has been inscribed in the given square A B C D.

10
00:01:24,136 --> 00:01:25,660
Which was to be done.

11
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:34,008
Proposition Four Eight completes Euclid's quartet of circle-and-square constructions by inscribing a circle in a given square.

12
00:01:34,008 --> 00:01:48,668
As Heath notes in his commentary, because a quadrilateral admits an inscribed circle whenever the sums of its opposite sides are equal, a circle can be inscribed in any square or rhombus, but never in an oblong rectangle or rhomboid.

13
00:01:48,668 --> 00:02:01,000
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty unites segment bisection, parallel lines, and parallelogram symmetry with the tangent criterion of Three Sixteen across forty-nine ancestor nodes.

14
